\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The boundary thickness floor holds on finite systems, fails under unbounded refinement, and coordinate sharing strictly lowers joint partition depth.} (\textbf  {A})~Computed---not assumed---floor $\beta $ across four finite systems spanning ten orders of magnitude, from a uniform weight set down to an adversarial instance whose smallest weight is $10^{-12}$. Every instance returns a strictly positive floor, and positivity does not depend on the scale of the weights. (\textbf  {B})~The failure branch. A system permitted to refine without limit generates thicknesses $1/n$ (orange): each is strictly positive and the infimum is zero. Truncating the same sequence to the first ten stages (blue) restores a positive floor at $0.1$ (dashed). The uniform floor therefore requires bounded refinement, and this chart exhibits the requirement rather than leaving it tacit. (\textbf  {C})~Bound partition depth $\mathcal  {M}_{\mathrm  {bound}}$ against free partition depth $\mathcal  {M}_{\mathrm  {free}}$ for four hundred randomly generated pairs of constituents sharing a coordinate. Every point falls on or below the identity (dashed): sharing never raises joint depth, and the scatter below the line is the deficit released as binding energy. (\textbf  {D})~Depth deficit $\Delta \mathcal  {M} = \log _2(\min (k_1,k_2))$ as a three-dimensional surface over the two constituents' individual branching factors $k_1$ and $k_2$. The deficit grows with the smaller of the two factors and is symmetric under exchange, since the shared coordinate can supply at most as much depth as the more finely specified constituent would otherwise have carried alone.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:respanel1}{{1}{5}{\textbf {The boundary thickness floor holds on finite systems, fails under unbounded refinement, and coordinate sharing strictly lowers joint partition depth.} (\textbf {A})~Computed---not assumed---floor $\beta $ across four finite systems spanning ten orders of magnitude, from a uniform weight set down to an adversarial instance whose smallest weight is $10^{-12}$. Every instance returns a strictly positive floor, and positivity does not depend on the scale of the weights. (\textbf {B})~The failure branch. A system permitted to refine without limit generates thicknesses $1/n$ (orange): each is strictly positive and the infimum is zero. Truncating the same sequence to the first ten stages (blue) restores a positive floor at $0.1$ (dashed). The uniform floor therefore requires bounded refinement, and this chart exhibits the requirement rather than leaving it tacit. (\textbf {C})~Bound partition depth $\mathcal {M}_{\mathrm {bound}}$ against free partition depth $\mathcal {M}_{\mathrm {free}}$ for four hundred randomly generated pairs of constituents sharing a coordinate. Every point falls on or below the identity (dashed): sharing never raises joint depth, and the scatter below the line is the deficit released as binding energy. (\textbf {D})~Depth deficit $\Delta \mathcal {M} = \log _2(\min (k_1,k_2))$ as a three-dimensional surface over the two constituents' individual branching factors $k_1$ and $k_2$. The deficit grows with the smaller of the two factors and is symmetric under exchange, since the shared coordinate can supply at most as much depth as the more finely specified constituent would otherwise have carried alone}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:respanel1@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][4][]5}{}{}{}}
