\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Point mutation in the real genetic code preferentially connects physicochemically similar residues, and codon degeneracy tracks structural simplicity and early evolutionary recruitment.} (\textbf  {A})~Density histograms of physicochemical distance (joint hydrophobicity/volume) for all amino acid pairs (orange) against pairs reachable from one another by a single base substitution in the standard genetic code (blue). The single-mutation distribution is shifted toward smaller distances, and this shift survives two thousand label-permutation shuffles of the codon table at $p=0.0005$. (\textbf  {B})~Codon degeneracy against heavy-atom count for the twenty standard residues. Degeneracy is highest among the smallest residues and falls to one or two codons among residues with eleven or more heavy atoms. (\textbf  {C})~Heavy-atom count against the Trifonov consensus chronological rank of recruitment into the genetic code, an independently published ordering over sixty methods. Earlier-recruited residues (left) are structurally simpler; the correlation is positive and survives permutation testing. (\textbf  {D})~The three quantities jointly, as a three-dimensional scatter of Trifonov rank, heavy-atom count and codon degeneracy, coloured by rank. Early, simple, and highly degenerate residues occupy one corner of the space and late, complex, sparsely encoded residues occupy the opposite corner, with no free parameter separating the two.}}{14}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:respanel3}{{3}{14}{\textbf {Point mutation in the real genetic code preferentially connects physicochemically similar residues, and codon degeneracy tracks structural simplicity and early evolutionary recruitment.} (\textbf {A})~Density histograms of physicochemical distance (joint hydrophobicity/volume) for all amino acid pairs (orange) against pairs reachable from one another by a single base substitution in the standard genetic code (blue). The single-mutation distribution is shifted toward smaller distances, and this shift survives two thousand label-permutation shuffles of the codon table at $p=0.0005$. (\textbf {B})~Codon degeneracy against heavy-atom count for the twenty standard residues. Degeneracy is highest among the smallest residues and falls to one or two codons among residues with eleven or more heavy atoms. (\textbf {C})~Heavy-atom count against the Trifonov consensus chronological rank of recruitment into the genetic code, an independently published ordering over sixty methods. Earlier-recruited residues (left) are structurally simpler; the correlation is positive and survives permutation testing. (\textbf {D})~The three quantities jointly, as a three-dimensional scatter of Trifonov rank, heavy-atom count and codon degeneracy, coloured by rank. Early, simple, and highly degenerate residues occupy one corner of the space and late, complex, sparsely encoded residues occupy the opposite corner, with no free parameter separating the two}{figure.3}{}}
