\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The genetically encoded alphabet is a small fraction of known amino acid space, and the ternary partition capacity needed to address it is reached at a shallow, fixed depth.} (\textbf  {A})~Counts, on a logarithmic axis, of the twenty standard residues, the two co-translationally recoded exceptions (selenocysteine, pyrrolysine), and the lower-bound estimate of five hundred amino acids known to occur in nature. The encoded alphabet occupies well under one-tenth of the known space. (\textbf  {B})~Ternary partition capacity $C(n)=2n^2$ against partition depth $n$. The dashed line marks twenty residues and the dotted line marks depth three, the shallowest depth at which capacity ($18$) approaches the alphabet size; by depth five, capacity has reached fifty. (\textbf  {C})~Codon degeneracy for all twenty standard residues, sorted from least to most degenerate. The distribution is markedly non-uniform, ranging from a single codon to six, a structure inherited from the code rather than imposed on it. (\textbf  {D})~Three-dimensional capacity surface $C(n)=2n^2$ over partition depth $n$ and reference base $b$. Capacity is independent of the base and grows quadratically with depth alone, confirming that the alphabet-crossing depth in panel~B is a property of the quadratic capacity law and not an artefact of a particular choice of encoding base.}}{18}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:respanel4}{{4}{18}{\textbf {The genetically encoded alphabet is a small fraction of known amino acid space, and the ternary partition capacity needed to address it is reached at a shallow, fixed depth.} (\textbf {A})~Counts, on a logarithmic axis, of the twenty standard residues, the two co-translationally recoded exceptions (selenocysteine, pyrrolysine), and the lower-bound estimate of five hundred amino acids known to occur in nature. The encoded alphabet occupies well under one-tenth of the known space. (\textbf {B})~Ternary partition capacity $C(n)=2n^2$ against partition depth $n$. The dashed line marks twenty residues and the dotted line marks depth three, the shallowest depth at which capacity ($18$) approaches the alphabet size; by depth five, capacity has reached fifty. (\textbf {C})~Codon degeneracy for all twenty standard residues, sorted from least to most degenerate. The distribution is markedly non-uniform, ranging from a single codon to six, a structure inherited from the code rather than imposed on it. (\textbf {D})~Three-dimensional capacity surface $C(n)=2n^2$ over partition depth $n$ and reference base $b$. Capacity is independent of the base and grows quadratically with depth alone, confirming that the alphabet-crossing depth in panel~B is a property of the quadratic capacity law and not an artefact of a particular choice of encoding base}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:respanel4@cref}{{[figure][4][]4}{[1][17][]18}{}{}{}}
\newlabel{def:probecapacity}{{14.2}{18}{Probe capacity}{theorem.14.2}{}}
\newlabel{def:probecapacity@cref}{{[theorem][2][14]14.2}{[1][17][]18}{}{}{}}
