\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Partition-depth deficit orders real catalytic rates and bond energetics, and a parameter-free categorical-distance prediction matches four independently measured substrates within one order of magnitude.} (\textbf  {A})~Real depth deficit $\Delta \mathcal  {M}$ against real rate constant, on a logarithmic axis, for five mechanistically distinct cytochrome P450 reaction types. The ordering is strictly monotonic: the NIH shift has both the smallest deficit and the fastest rate, and desaturation has both the largest deficit and the slowest. (\textbf  {B})~Real alpha-carbon bond dissociation energy against real activation energy for three heteroatom-dealkylation pathways. The two quantities are in strict agreement of order (N below O below aliphatic), and a weaker bond corresponds to a lower activation requirement throughout. (\textbf  {C})~Measured catalytic efficiency $\log _{10}(k_{\mathrm  {cat}}/K_M)$ for four real CYP3A4 substrates, each requiring the same categorical distance $d_C=4$, against a single parameter-free prediction of $10^{6}$~M$^{-1}$s$^{-1}$ (dashed). All four measurements fall within one order of magnitude of the prediction despite the substrates' otherwise distinct chemistry. (\textbf  {D})~Three-dimensional surface fitted through the three real dealkylation anchor points relating bond dissociation energy, activation energy and depth deficit, with the anchor points themselves marked as open circles. The surface is monotonic in both energetic axes, consistent with depth deficit tracking bond strength through the activation energy it sets.}}{19}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:respanel6}{{5}{19}{\textbf {Partition-depth deficit orders real catalytic rates and bond energetics, and a parameter-free categorical-distance prediction matches four independently measured substrates within one order of magnitude.} (\textbf {A})~Real depth deficit $\Delta \mathcal {M}$ against real rate constant, on a logarithmic axis, for five mechanistically distinct cytochrome P450 reaction types. The ordering is strictly monotonic: the NIH shift has both the smallest deficit and the fastest rate, and desaturation has both the largest deficit and the slowest. (\textbf {B})~Real alpha-carbon bond dissociation energy against real activation energy for three heteroatom-dealkylation pathways. The two quantities are in strict agreement of order (N below O below aliphatic), and a weaker bond corresponds to a lower activation requirement throughout. (\textbf {C})~Measured catalytic efficiency $\log _{10}(k_{\mathrm {cat}}/K_M)$ for four real CYP3A4 substrates, each requiring the same categorical distance $d_C=4$, against a single parameter-free prediction of $10^{6}$~M$^{-1}$s$^{-1}$ (dashed). All four measurements fall within one order of magnitude of the prediction despite the substrates' otherwise distinct chemistry. (\textbf {D})~Three-dimensional surface fitted through the three real dealkylation anchor points relating bond dissociation energy, activation energy and depth deficit, with the anchor points themselves marked as open circles. The surface is monotonic in both energetic axes, consistent with depth deficit tracking bond strength through the activation energy it sets}{figure.5}{}}
\newlabel{fig:respanel6@cref}{{[figure][5][]5}{[1][18][]19}{}{}{}}
