\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The alphabet-bounded resolution ceiling is far below the depth needed to address an actual fold by position, probe capacity predicts real metabolic coupling, and chemically dissimilar cofactors converge in partition depth across a tightly coupled electron-transport segment.} (\textbf  {A})~Partition depth required to address membership in the twenty-two-residue alphabet, $3.00$, against the partition depth required to address one of 503 individual fold positions in the real cytochrome CYP3A4 structure, $5.66$: the two quantities are governed independently by alphabet size and by chain length respectively, and the store can only ever supply the first. (\textbf  {B})~Real substrate-promiscuity index $\sigma $ against the real fraction of clinically used drugs metabolised, for three human cytochrome P450 isoforms. The isoform of highest promiscuity, CYP3A4, also accounts for the largest drug-metabolising share, exactly as predicted by usefulness being bounded by probe capacity. (\textbf  {C})~Independently computed partition depth $\mathcal  {M}$ for four cofactors along a real four-hop electron transport chain (NADPH, FAD, FMN, the haem iron centre). The three flavoprotein-segment cofactors cluster tightly in depth despite their distinct chemistry; the haem centre, reached only after the longer inter-protein hop, sits apart. (\textbf  {D})~Three-dimensional scatter of the real partition addresses of fifty-seven human cytochrome P450 isoforms, plotted as prefix sum, suffix sum and total address depth, coloured by family. Isoforms of the same family cluster along the address-depth axis, the direct geometric expression of the cross-lineage recurrence the alphabet itself was built from.}}{25}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:respanel5}{{6}{25}{\textbf {The alphabet-bounded resolution ceiling is far below the depth needed to address an actual fold by position, probe capacity predicts real metabolic coupling, and chemically dissimilar cofactors converge in partition depth across a tightly coupled electron-transport segment.} (\textbf {A})~Partition depth required to address membership in the twenty-two-residue alphabet, $3.00$, against the partition depth required to address one of 503 individual fold positions in the real cytochrome CYP3A4 structure, $5.66$: the two quantities are governed independently by alphabet size and by chain length respectively, and the store can only ever supply the first. (\textbf {B})~Real substrate-promiscuity index $\sigma $ against the real fraction of clinically used drugs metabolised, for three human cytochrome P450 isoforms. The isoform of highest promiscuity, CYP3A4, also accounts for the largest drug-metabolising share, exactly as predicted by usefulness being bounded by probe capacity. (\textbf {C})~Independently computed partition depth $\mathcal {M}$ for four cofactors along a real four-hop electron transport chain (NADPH, FAD, FMN, the haem iron centre). The three flavoprotein-segment cofactors cluster tightly in depth despite their distinct chemistry; the haem centre, reached only after the longer inter-protein hop, sits apart. (\textbf {D})~Three-dimensional scatter of the real partition addresses of fifty-seven human cytochrome P450 isoforms, plotted as prefix sum, suffix sum and total address depth, coloured by family. Isoforms of the same family cluster along the address-depth axis, the direct geometric expression of the cross-lineage recurrence the alphabet itself was built from}{figure.6}{}}
\newlabel{fig:respanel5@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][24][]25}{}{}{}}
